<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p40_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized with the Brain positioned at the top left corner, shaded gray with a dotted pattern, and a specific region labeled \"Hypothalamus.\" The Hypothalamus is represented by a small triangular shape connected to the Brain with a curved arrow labeled \"TSH\" pointing towards the Pituitary Gland. The Pituitary Gland is a small oval with a dotted pattern, linked by a leader line from the Hypothalamus, and it also has a curved arrow labeled \"TSH\" directed towards the Thyroid Gland. The Thyroid Gland is depicted as two connected rounded shapes, with a leader line extending from the Pituitary Gland. On the right side, a large pinkish area labeled \"TISSUES\" occupies the space, with the word \"TISSUES\" written in red at the bottom. Arrows labeled \"5' Deiodinase,\" \"T4,\" \"RT3,\" and \"T3\" illustrate the flow from the Thyroid Gland to the Tissues and a Cell. The Cell is a circular shape with a dotted pattern, and there is a curved arrow from the Tissues pointing towards it. On the right side of the image, a box lists factors that inhibit the process: \"Stress,\" \"Fasting,\" \"Illness,\" and \"Cortisol.\"" <urn:graph:thyroid_source> .
