<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p45_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized with the plasma membrane at the top left, represented as a double-layered structure with a wavy pattern. Near this membrane, an orange circle labeled \"S\" for steroid hormone is shown. Below the plasma membrane, the cytoplasm is depicted in yellow and contains a purple, crescent-shaped structure labeled \"Receptor protein.\" Further down, a green, elongated structure labeled \"Protein synthesis\" is positioned, likely representing a ribosome. At the bottom left, the nucleus is shown in pink, containing a coiled structure labeled \"Chromosome\" and a single-stranded RNA molecule labeled \"mRNA\" extending into the cytoplasm. Red arrows guide the process: one from the steroid hormone to the receptor protein, another from the receptor protein to the nucleus, a third from the nucleus to the mRNA, and finally, one from the mRNA to the protein synthesis area. On the right side, above the plasma membrane, a text box states that steroid hormones are lipid soluble and pass through the plasma membrane readily. Below it, another box explains that inside the target cell, the steroid hormone binds to a specific receptor protein." <urn:graph:thyroid_source> .
