<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p52_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "A diagram is presented with a red box at the top-left labeled \"HOMEOSTASIS DISTURBED,\" followed by a white section with black text that reads, \"Decreased T3, T4 concentration in blood or low body temperature.\" Central to the diagram is a large yellowish-orange structure labeled \"Anterior pituitary,\" above which a smaller structure labeled \"Hypothalamus\" is positioned. An arrow connects the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary, indicating the release of TRH (Thyrotropin-Releasing Hormone). To the right of the anterior pituitary, a blue box labeled \"Anterior pituitary releases TSH\" is placed, with an arrow pointing from the anterior pituitary to another instance of itself. Below the anterior pituitary, a light brown structure labeled \"Thyroid gland\" is shown, with an arrow indicating the release of TSH from the anterior pituitary to the thyroid gland. The thyroid gland releases T3 and T4, represented by hexagonal shapes with their names above them. At the bottom-left, a blue box labeled \"HOMEOSTASIS RESTORED\" is positioned, followed by a white section with black text that reads, \"Increased T3 and T4 concentration in the blood.\" A green box at the bottom-center states, \"Thyroid follicles release T3 and T4,\" with arrows pointing from the thyroid gland to the thyroid follicles, indicating the release of these hormones." <urn:graph:thyroid_source> .
