<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p30_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized with the Hypothalamus at the top left, connected by an arrow pointing downward to the Thyrotrope region below. The Thyrotrope is labeled with \"TRH\" (Thyroid-Releasing Hormone), shown as an arrow coming from the Hypothalamus. The Thyroid Gland is a large, irregularly shaped structure occupying most of the lower portion of the image, with its label placed to the right. Within the Thyroid Gland, two main receptor structures are depicted: a blue receptor labeled \"G Protein\" and a green receptor labeled \"Adenylyl cyclase,\" connected by arrows indicating their interaction. The pathway shows the conversion of ATP into cAMP. A gray box labeled \"Inactive protein kinase A\" is shown, with cAMP binding to it, activating it to become \"Active protein kinase A.\" The activated PKA leads to protein phosphorylation, culminating in the secretion of thyroid hormones (T4 and T3) from the Thyroid Gland." <urn:graph:thyroid_source> .
