<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p34_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The diagram is organized into three main sections, each representing a part of the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis. At the top, the hypothalamus is depicted as a gray rectangular area with the label \"Hypothalamus\" above it and \"TRH\" below, indicating its role in producing Thyrotropin-Releasing Hormone. Below the hypothalamus, a dashed horizontal line separates it from the anterior pituitary, which is shown as a yellow rectangular area. The label \"Anterior Pituitary\" is placed above this section, with \"TSH\" written below, signifying the production of Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone. A gray circle within this yellow area likely represents the anterior pituitary gland. Another dashed horizontal line separates the anterior pituitary from the thyroid gland, located at the bottom. The thyroid gland is represented by a larger yellow rectangular area with the label \"Thyroid Gland\" above and \"T3 and T4\" below, indicating the production of Triiodothyronine and Thyroxine. Two gray circles within this area likely symbolize the thyroid gland. Arrows or lines connect these structures, illustrating the hormonal interactions." <urn:graph:thyroid_source> .
