<http://example.org/medkg/inst/fig/p19_b1> <http://example.org/medkg/ont#visualDescription> "The image is organized into sections, with the top left showing a chemical reaction starting with an iodide ion (I⁻) that is oxidized to an iodine atom (I°). Below this, a structure labeled \"TYR\" represents tyrosine, featuring a benzene ring with a hydroxyl group. In the middle section, two structures labeled \"MIT\" and \"DIT\" are displayed, where MIT has one iodine atom and DIT has two on their benzene rings. Both MIT and DIT are shown reacting with another DIT molecule, leading to the formation of T3 and T4. On the right, the final products T3 and T4 are detailed, with T3 formed by the reaction of MIT and DIT, and T4 by the reaction of DIT and DIT. The image uses arrows to guide the direction of these reactions and labels to identify each intermediate and final product." <urn:graph:thyroid_source> .
